---
---

<section class="industry">
  <div class="container industry__split">
    <div class="industry__media" data-reveal data-parallax-wrap data-industry-media>
      <picture>
        <source srcset="/images/industry-expertise.avif" type="image/avif" />
        <img
          class="industry__media-img industry__media-img--1"
          src="/images/industry-expertise.jpg"
          alt=""
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
          data-parallax-image
        />
      </picture>
      <picture>
        <source srcset="/images/industry-professionals.avif" type="image/avif" />
        <img
          class="industry__media-img industry__media-img--2"
          src="/images/industry-professionals.jpg"
          alt=""
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
          data-parallax-image
        />
      </picture>
      <picture>
        <source srcset="/images/clear-horizon.avif" type="image/avif" />
        <img
          class="industry__media-img industry__media-img--3"
          src="/images/clear-horizon.jpg"
          alt=""
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
          data-parallax-image
        />
      </picture>
    </div>

    <div class="industry__text">
      <div class="industry__block" data-reveal>
        <div class="industry__block-media">
          <picture>
            <source srcset="/images/industry-expertise.avif" type="image/avif" />
            <img src="/images/industry-expertise.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          </picture>
        </div>
        <h2 class="industry__heading" data-reveal-heading>Industry Expertise</h2>
        <p class="industry__body">
          We understand the challenges faced by network operators and the expertise required to
          drive operational success. That's why CHIME was developed with insights from professionals
          across the industry, ensuring it meets the real-world needs of those working on the front
          lines.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="industry__block" data-reveal data-industry-frame="2">
        <div class="industry__block-media">
          <picture>
            <source srcset="/images/industry-professionals.avif" type="image/avif" />
            <img src="/images/industry-professionals.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          </picture>
        </div>
        <h2 class="industry__heading" data-reveal-heading>Industry Professionals</h2>
        <p class="industry__body">
          At the heart of every successful network operation are the dedicated professionals who
          bring expertise, precision, and innovation to their roles. From design and construction to
          maintenance and oversight, these specialists ensure the seamless function and evolution of
          critical infrastructure.
        </p>
        <p class="industry__body">
          CHIME is built on the collective experience of these industry experts, offering solutions
          to those shaping the future of network operations.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="industry__block" data-reveal data-industry-frame="3">
        <div class="industry__block-media">
          <picture>
            <source srcset="/images/clear-horizon.avif" type="image/avif" />
            <img src="/images/clear-horizon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          </picture>
        </div>
        <h2 class="industry__heading" data-reveal-heading>Clear Horizon</h2>
        <p class="industry__body">
          Our team specialises in the business of regulated network operators. We have consultants
          with over 20 years industry experience combined with highly skilled business analysts,
          solution design and secure enterprise IT service architecture. Our reputation at the most
          senior levels in the industry is built on the strength of our exceptional team.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .industry {
    background: var(--color-white);
  }

  /* Narrower than the page's own container (global.css) — this section
     reads as a contained two-column block, not a full-width band. */
  .industry__split {
    max-width: 68rem;
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: 4rem;
    padding-block: 7rem;
  }

  /* Centring the sticky point (top: 50vh below) makes it lock as soon as
     the image reaches the vertical middle of the viewport - with no
     buffer beforehand, that point arrives while AcceleratingProductivity's
     card above is still partly on screen, since its bottom edge and this
     column's top are the same scroll position. This extra top padding
     (desktop only - matches the breakpoint below sticky becomes static)
     delays that lock point until AcceleratingProductivity has fully
     scrolled away first. */
  @media (min-width: 900px) {
    .industry__split {
      padding-top: 55vh;
    }
  }

  /* Sticky while the (now taller, three-block) text column scrolls past
     it - align-items: flex-start above keeps this column at its own
     natural height instead of stretching to match the text column,
     which is what gives the sticky effect room to work. The top offset
     is computed directly (50vh minus half this element's own height:
     28.75rem wide at aspect-ratio 4/5 = 35.9375rem tall, so 17.96875rem)
     rather than using top: 50% + transform: translateY(-50%) - combining
     position: sticky with a transform on the same element is a known
     cross-browser quirk where the sticky offset can miscalculate when
     the scroll direction reverses (confirmed: image stuck at the top
     the entire time scrolling up, fine scrolling down). A plain top
     value avoids that entirely while landing at the exact same
     centred position. */
  .industry__media {
    position: sticky;
    top: calc(50vh - 17.96875rem);
    flex: 0 0 28.75rem;
    aspect-ratio: 4 / 5;
    border-radius: 24px;
    overflow: hidden;
    background: linear-gradient(160deg, var(--color-grey-100), #dce9f2 55%, #c9e4f0);
  }

  /* Sized 116% with a matching negative inset so the scroll-parallax drift
     (--parallax-y, from image-parallax.ts) never uncovers the frame's
     edges. max-width: none overrides
     global.css's site-wide `img { max-width: 100% }` reset, which was
     silently capping the width back down to 100% (height had no such
     reset, so only it was actually reaching 116%) - that's what left
     gaps down the sides showing the container's own background through. */
  .industry__media-img {
    position: absolute;
    inset: -8%;
    width: 116%;
    max-width: none;
    height: 116%;
    object-fit: cover;
    transform: translateY(var(--parallax-y, 0px));
    transition: opacity 0.6s ease;
  }

  .industry__media-img--1 {
    opacity: 1;
  }

  .industry__media-img--2,
  .industry__media-img--3 {
    opacity: 0;
  }

  /* Toggled by industry-crossfade.ts as each text block scrolls into
     view - mutually exclusive, so only one image is ever opacity: 1. */
  .industry__media.is-frame-2 .industry__media-img--1 {
    opacity: 0;
  }

  .industry__media.is-frame-2 .industry__media-img--2 {
    opacity: 1;
  }

  .industry__media.is-frame-3 .industry__media-img--1 {
    opacity: 0;
  }

  .industry__media.is-frame-3 .industry__media-img--3 {
    opacity: 1;
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .industry__media-img {
      transition: none;
    }
  }

  .industry__text {
    flex: 1;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 24rem;
  }

  /* Every block gets a min-height matching the sticky image beside them
     (28.75rem wide × 4:5 aspect-ratio = 35.9375rem tall) with their text
     centred inside it. For the second and third blocks this also means
     the text column stays tall enough that the image's sticky lock
     doesn't release until that block's text is centred in the viewport
     too, rather than the image scrolling away right as it appears. */
  .industry__block {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 1.25rem;
    min-height: 35.9375rem;
  }

  .industry__heading {
    font-size: 2rem;
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .industry__body {
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--color-grey-600);
  }

  /* Desktop-only stand-in for the per-block image below: hidden here
     since the shared sticky industry__media (crossfading between all
     three photos as you scroll) already shows each one at the right
     moment. */
  .industry__block-media {
    display: none;
  }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .industry__split {
      flex-direction: column;
      align-items: stretch;
    }

    /* 24rem was the desktop scroll-runway distance between blocks (room
       for the sticky image to sit still while each one scrolls past) -
       with no sticky effect on mobile, and each block now carrying its
       own image, that gap is just an enormous empty space between
       otherwise self-contained image+text groups. */
    .industry__text {
      gap: 3.5rem;
    }

    /* The shared sticky media box only makes sense with the desktop
       row/sticky layout - on mobile there's no scroll-driven pinning, so
       it would just sit once at the top of the whole section showing
       frame 1 forever (frames 2/3 only ever meant to be revealed via the
       crossfade while it stays locked next to whichever block is in
       view). Hiding it entirely and showing each block's own image
       instead, so every block still gets a photo where it actually is. */
    .industry__media {
      display: none;
    }

    .industry__block-media {
      display: block;
      aspect-ratio: 16 / 10;
      border-radius: 24px;
      overflow: hidden;
    }

    .industry__block-media img {
      width: 100%;
      height: 100%;
      object-fit: cover;
    }

    .industry__block {
      min-height: 0;
    }
  }
</style>
